  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "danish-folk-music-tunes-forms-and-style",
      "title": "Danish Folk Music — Tunes, Forms, and Style",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** Nordic Folk | **Region:** nordic-denmark | **Tier:** 4\n**Prerequisites:** regions-nordic-denmark-03-instruments-voices-ii\n**Tags:** nordic, denmark, nordic, tunes,, forms,"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "reels, pols, waltzes\n\nDanish Folk Music belongs to the **Nordic Folk** curriculum. Harboe Brothers\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **reels, pols, waltzes** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-norway",
      "title": "Compared with Norway",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Norwegian Folk Music** (`regions-nordic-norway-04-genres-forms`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **reels, pols, waltzes**.\n\n| Topic | Norway anchor | Danish Folk Music (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | slått, pols, halling… | reels, pols, waltzes… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-nordic-norway-04-genres-forms`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-04-genres-forms`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "reels, pols, waltzes\n\nStudy **danish folk music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Music of Denmark](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Denmark) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **Nordic Folk**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **fiddle, accordion**. Ensemble layer: **guitar, bass**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **reels, pols, waltzes**. Dance context: **folk dance**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **Danish collectors**. Contemporary scenes: **Tønder Festival**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Danish regional tunes**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- reels, pols, waltzes\n- Lead instruments: fiddle, accordion\n- Ensemble: guitar, bass\n- Forms: reels, pols, waltzes\n- Dance: folk dance\n- History: Danish collectors\n- Scenes: Tønder Festival\n- Repertoire: Danish regional tunes\n- Compared with Norway: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Danish Folk Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: reels, pols, waltzes.\n- Compared with Norway: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: reels, pols, waltzes.\n- Dance: folk dance.\n- Transmission: Danish collectors."
        }
      ]
    },
